WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.340
Geostorm failed to find sunny skies at the
box office over its opening weekend, pulling

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:12.120
in just $13.3 million domestic against a reported
budget of over $120 million.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.120
The disaster thriller, starring Gerard Butler
as a scientist tasked with fixing satellites

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.890
protecting the world from the catastrophic
effects of climate change, was the latest

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:26.830
in a string of big-budget flops in 2017, knocked
down by a rocky production and a chilly reception

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:27.820
from critics.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:29.900
"It's worse than we thought."

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.340
"Yeah, I think we've established that."

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.480
Still, there's a lot more to what went wrong
with Geostorm than just the obvious.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.460
Here are the real reasons why Geostorm flopped
at the box office.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:40.980
Stormy skies

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.340
Geostorm took a long time to make its way
to theaters.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:49.269
The movie was initially sold to Warner Bros.
in 2014, with a release date set for March

00:00:49.269 --> 00:00:50.360
of 2016.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:56.580
But then the release was bumped to October
of 2016, then to January of 2017, and finally

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.260
its eventual October of 2017 release.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:04.520
A lot happened in the interim to hurt Geostorm's
chances of success, including some production

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:08.180
hurdles that drove up the cost—and generated
bad word of mouth.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.880
Sources say that debut director Dean Devlin's
first cut was "unwatchable," and despite weeks

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.549
of post-production and numerous other test
screenings, things didn't appear to get any

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:19.049
better.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.260
"It's gotta be a glitch."

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:24.600
Devlin reportedly became overwhelmed with
the movie's scale, and struggled to get it

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.460
ready on time.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:30.660
So, with the director's blessing, the studio
brought on mega-producer Jerry Bruckheimer

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:37.039
to oversee some $15 million in reshoots under
director Danny Canon and screenwriter Laeta

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:37.640
Kalogridis.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.980
The reshoots removed some characters, looking
to better tie together the film's already

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:45.200
existing VFX sequences in a way that made
sense.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.600
Although reports say that the film tested
much better after the edits, the added time

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.680
and cost and the bad press that came with
it didn't help.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.380
Been there, done that

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.320
Another problem with Geostorm is that disaster
movies are hardly new.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.100
In recent years, there've been a bevy of similar
projects, like San Andreas and Deepwater Horizon,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.040
to name a few.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:09.300
And while some have seen success for being
in on their own jokes, like the Sharknado

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.180
series, Geostorm was trying to be completely
serious and faced stiffer competition as a

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:14.700
result.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.640
Devlin himself help ed to crowd the field
further when he set up the Independence Day

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.640
sequel Resurgence, the lackluster reception
of which signified audience fatigue with disaster

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.620
movies—something Geostorm's disappointing
performance seems to confirm.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.260
Bad buzz

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.860
The audience's disinterest in turning out
for Geostorm was reflected in the movie's

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.460
social media conversation.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:40.340
Analysts noted that most of the conversation
about the film online was negative, with viewers

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.780
comparing it to films like Armageddon and
The Day After Tomorrow.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:48.200
Audiences have been getting more discerning
recently about reboots and remakes, and even

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:52.799
though Geostorm isn't officially based on
a past property, the fact that the movie draws

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.910
so heavily on what's come before—without
appearing to offer much that's new—was enough

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.380
to keep audiences at home.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.220
The film was also largely maligned with critics.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.800
The studio decided not to screen the film
for reviewers and even held back on offering

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.260
Thursday night screenings to protect the movie's
potential for a Friday night haul.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.080
However, nothing could prevent the movie from
eventually landing in the tough hands of critics,

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.920
who absolutely tore it apart.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:22.420
Geostorm currently sits at a dismal 11 percent
rating on Rotten Tomatoes, with critics knocking

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:24.860
the movie for being "big, dumb and boring."

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.720
That certainly couldn't have helped draw in
anyone who was on the fence about seeing it

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:29.740
in the first place.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.460
Marketing mayhem

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.360
Marketing plays a huge part in the chances
of a movie's success, but if Geostorm's PR

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.459
campaign telegraphed anything to viewers,
it was the fact that the film was an absolute

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:40.960
mess.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.600
This was mainly shown in the huge difference
in tone between the film's first and second

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:46.100
trailers.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:51.120
The first Geostorm trailer was a somber affair,
beginning quiet and eerie before underlining

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.020
some terrifying and deadly climate-related
events.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:58.549
The second, though, was more akin to a disaster
comedy, using the same clips but with much

00:03:58.549 --> 00:04:03.189
more light-hearted music and some quips from
Gerard Butler and his co-stars to make the

00:04:03.189 --> 00:04:07.620
typhoons and earthquakes seem more like a
fun romp than a catastrophic, world ending

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:08.120
event.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.140
"I literally had to fly in from outer space."

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:15.819
A lot of this probably came about thanks to
the movie's extensive reshoots, but the fact

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:20.880
that the two trailers were selling entirely
different films wasn't a good sign for a potential

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:21.380
audience.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:22.840
Too soon

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.900
Geostorm was also the victim of some pretty
bad timing, arriving in theaters as the nation

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.180
was still reeling from the effects of three
devastating hurricanes, as well as deadly

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:35.760
earthquakes in Mexico and catastrophic flooding
around the globe.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.320
With so much disaster happening in real life,
it's easy to understand why audiences might

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.340
want to avoid seeing it on the big screen.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:47.580
Geostorm's marketers recognized this, and
pulled posters featuring the movie's old tagline,

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.460
"Brave the Storm," replacing them with a new
tagline reading "Control the Weather, Control

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:53.060
the World."

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.360
However, that doesn't appear to have been
enough, as the movie underperformed in regions

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.020
that had been hit by the hurricanes.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.760
While there's some debate as to how much outside
events affect a movie's box office performance,

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:08.170
the fact that so much of the movie's plot
was reminiscent of real life tragedies likely

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:09.900
kept at least some viewers away.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:11.380
Thanks for watching!

